  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface high pressure will continue building in from the west \nthis evening, ushering in drier and cooler air tonight with a \nmostly clear sky. Areas of valley fog are expected to develop \novernight/early tomorrow morning. Lows tonight will be running \nseveral degrees below average in the mid 40s to the lower 50s. \n\nThe surface high settles overhead tomorrow setting up a nice \nstart to the weekend. After the morning fog burns off, we'll be \nunder a mostly sunny sky with highs generally in the mid to \nupper 70s. With dew points in the 50s, it will be a very \ncomfortable day. \n\nSunday is expected to trend warmer as the high starts to shift \neast and the flow becomes more west-southwesterly with highs in \nthe upper 70s and lower 80s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA zonal flow pattern sets up next week with several weak waves \npushing across the region. A surface frontal boundary dips south\nout of Canada Sunday night into Monday and may stall near the \narea. This could lead to multiple rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms through the middle of next week, along with \nseveral shortwaves that push through the zonal flow pattern. \n\nSome deterministic model guidance does show the zonal flow \nbecoming more amplified with a stronger upper level ridge \nbuilding over the Tennessee River Valley Tuesday into Wednesday \nwhich could set up the potential for some locally robust storms \nmidweek depending on timing and placement of any impulses aloft,\nas temperatures would trend a bit warmer and dew points could \nrise well into the 60s. Ensemble guidance does seem to keep the \n500mb flow more zonal during this time, so it's a trend to \nwatch. \n\nHigh temperatures Monday are expected to be in the upper 70s \nand low 80s, while Tuesday and Wednesday range from the upper \n70s to the mid 80s. Overnight lows will be in the 60s during \nthis time.",
